Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero and Ezero compiler toolkit for Linux x86-64

This portable package contains both original compilers, their source, Ezero's
original runtime, examples and user-level launchers. The native executables
use Linux x86-64 system calls directly. They have no dynamic loader, libc,
Python, GCC, LLVM or separately installed language-runtime dependency.
Shell launchers/build scripts require Bash and ordinary GNU core utilities.
These are host orchestration; language parsing/execution is implemented in Zero.

Extract anywhere, including a directory whose path contains spaces:
    tar -xzf zero-ezero-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
    cd zero-ezero-linux-x86_64
    ./bin/ezero run share/ezero/examples/banana.ezero
    ./bin/zero run share/ezero/examples/hello.zero

Keep a compiled application and generated Zero source:
    ./bin/ezero build share/ezero/examples/banana.ezero banana --emit-zero banana.zero
    ./banana
    ./bin/zero build banana.zero another-banana

Generate only the Zero source:
    ./bin/ezero emit-zero share/ezero/examples/banana.ezero banana.zero

The original Zero stdin/stdout compiler interface remains available:
    ./bin/zero --host < program.zero > program
    chmod +x program
    ./program
Zero's raw/firmware target switches are also passed through unchanged.
The high-level build/run conveniences target hosted Linux x86-64.

Optional user-level installation, with no administrator privileges:
    bash install.sh
This copies the toolkit to $HOME/.local/share/zero-ezero and installs zero and
ezero launchers in $HOME/.local/bin. Put that bin directory in your PATH.
For a separate prefix:
    bash install.sh --prefix /absolute/path/to/my-tools
Existing destinations are refused, never silently replaced. The unpacked
package remains usable without installation. Installed symlinks are relative
to the selected prefix, and launchers locate their data independently of cwd.

Reproduce all three native compiler/tool binaries with our supplied compiler:
    mkdir rebuilt
    bash rebuild.sh rebuilt
This recompiles Zero twice, checks byte-identical self-reproduction, compiles
Ezero's frontend and package writer, and compares all three shipped binaries.
No external compiler or assembler is used.

Run portable-package verification:
    bash tests/smoke.sh
It exercises all public examples, both languages, emitted source, paths with
spaces, failed-build preservation, source rebuilds and user-prefix installation.
Tests use temporary directories and do not access the OS disk images.
BUILD-INPUTS.sha256 identifies the project source snapshot used for this bundle.
SHA256SUMS checks the complete package; it is not a cryptographic signature.

Compatibility
The target ABI is Linux x86-64, including Debian and Arch Linux. No
distribution-specific package manager or shared-library version is required.
The actual distribution test matrix is recorded in the accompanying release
verification report; compatibility should not be confused with an unperformed
distribution boot/test. Non-x86-64 CPUs and non-Linux kernels are not targets
of this archive. At least 8 MiB of process stack must be available; generated
host programs reserve a 4 MiB arena on their stack.

Language and execution boundaries
Ezero text normally needs no quotes. UTF-8 phrases retain numeric and text
parts, and collection indices begin at 1. See share/ezero/LANGUAGE.ztext.
Generated Ezero applications embed our own bounded instruction interpreter
and garbage collector. They are standalone binaries, not direct native
lowering of each Ezero expression. Current library features and limits are
documented alongside the source. This Linux package makes no claim about
guest Zero OS or native Windows execution; those are separate integrations.
